window.PREVIEW_MOCKS["nimhans-2026-recall"] = {"id": "nimhans-2026-recall", "title": "NIMHANS 2026 Recall: your 9 + cluster", "fullTitle": "NIMHANS 2026 Recall (Qs 36-41, tamas, dharana, Kegels from your 31 May paper + 18 cluster Qs)", "sub": "27 questions · your 9 recalled NIMHANS 31 May Qs + 18 cluster Qs · untimed", "sectionLabels": {"recall": "Your 9 from NIMHANS 31 May", "cluster": "The cluster around them"}, "questions": [{"q": "Which type of defence mechanism is dissociation?", "options": {"a": "Mature", "b": "Immature", "c": "Neurotic", "d": "Conscious"}, "correct": "c", "section": "recall", "explanation": "The answer is neurotic. The ranking comes from George Vaillant, the American psychiatrist who ran the Harvard Grant Study of adult development. In Adaptation to Life (1977) he took the defences that Sigmund Freud and Anna Freud (The Ego and the Mechanisms of Defence, 1936) had described and arranged them by maturity into four levels. Level I, psychotic: delusional projection, psychotic denial of external reality and distortion, where reality testing is lost. Level II, immature: projection, schizoid fantasy, hypochondriasis, passive aggression and acting out, normal in adolescence and common in personality disorders. Level III, neurotic (later called intermediate): repression, displacement, reaction formation, intellectualization (with isolation and undoing) and dissociation, which Vaillant also called neurotic denial. Level IV, mature: altruism, humour, sublimation, suppression and anticipation. Dissociation sits at the neurotic level because it is a temporary, drastic change in one's sense of identity or feeling to escape distress, while reality testing stays intact and the person keeps functioning. In the Grant Study, men who relied on mature defences had better health, marriages and careers decades later. The DSM-IV Defensive Functioning Scale (1994) keeps the same placement under another name: dissociation is at the mental inhibitions (compromise formation) level, with repression, displacement, intellectualization, isolation of affect, reaction formation and undoing. Hold the ladder with one anchor per level: distortion is psychotic, projection is immature, dissociation and repression are neurotic, suppression and sublimation are mature.", "topic": "Q36 · Vaillant: dissociation is a neurotic defence"}, {"q": "A technique in which a response that inhibits anxiety is paired with an anxiety-provoking stimulus, step by step, to break the conditioned link between that stimulus and anxiety is:", "options": {"a": "Systematic desensitization", "b": "Flooding", "c": "Aversion therapy", "d": "Exposure and response prevention"}, "correct": "a", "section": "recall", "explanation": "The answer is systematic desensitization, built by the South African psychiatrist Joseph Wolpe and set out in Psychotherapy by Reciprocal Inhibition (1958). Wolpe started with cats: he made them anxious with mild shocks in an experimental cage, then removed the anxiety by feeding them in rooms that looked less and less like that cage, so eating, a response incompatible with anxiety, gradually won. He borrowed Charles Sherrington's term reciprocal inhibition for the principle: if a response that inhibits anxiety is made in the presence of the anxiety-provoking stimulus, the bond between that stimulus and anxiety weakens. In people the incompatible response is usually deep muscle relaxation, taken from Edmund Jacobson's progressive relaxation (1938); Wolpe also used assertive and sexual responses. There are three steps. First, relaxation training. Second, an anxiety hierarchy: ten to twenty scenes ranked from least to most frightening on the Subjective Units of Disturbance scale (SUDS, 0 to 100). Third, desensitization proper: while relaxed, the client imagines the lowest scene, signals if anxiety rises, returns to relaxation, and moves up only when a scene can be held calmly. This is counterconditioning, replacing the conditioned response, not just waiting for extinction. The forerunner is Mary Cover Jones (1924), who removed little Peter's fear of a rabbit by bringing the rabbit nearer while he ate. In vivo desensitization uses the real stimulus instead of imagined scenes. Stem words that point here: gradual, hierarchy, relaxation, incompatible response, counterconditioning.", "topic": "Q37 · Systematic desensitization (Wolpe)"}, {"q": "In habit reversal training (HRT), the technique used to make the person aware of the habit is:", "options": {"a": "Competing response training", "b": "Social support", "c": "Generalization training", "d": "Awareness training"}, "correct": "d", "section": "recall", "explanation": "The answer is awareness training, the first component of habit reversal training, which Nathan Azrin and Gregory Nunn published in 1973 ('Habit-reversal: a method of eliminating nervous habits and tics'). The logic is that tics, hair pulling, nail biting and skin picking run on autopilot, and you cannot block a habit you do not notice. Awareness training has four parts. Response description: the client describes the habit in detail, every movement, sometimes in front of a mirror. Response detection: the therapist points out each occurrence in session until the client catches every one himself. Early warning: the client learns to spot the earliest sign, the urge or the first movement (for hair pulling, the hand starting to rise toward the head). Situation awareness: listing the places, times, moods and people linked to the habit. Once the habit is caught early, the client uses the competing response, a physically incompatible action such as clenching the fists, held until the urge passes. Azrin and Nunn's original package had about a dozen techniques in four phases: awareness training, competing response training, habit control motivation (inconvenience review, social support, public display) and generalization training. Raymond Miltenberger, Wayne Fuqua and Douglas Woods (1998) showed that a simplified HRT works with just three: awareness training, competing response training and social support. The Comprehensive Behavioral Intervention for Tics (CBIT; John Piacentini and colleagues, 2010) is HRT plus function-based work and is a first-line treatment for Tourette's.", "topic": "Q38 · Habit reversal: awareness training"}, {"q": "The subjective confidence people have in their flashbulb memories is:", "options": {"a": "Low, because the event was emotionally shocking", "b": "Very high, and it stays high even as accuracy falls", "c": "Moderate, and in line with how accurate the memory is", "d": "High at first, then falls in step with accuracy"}, "correct": "b", "section": "recall", "explanation": "The answer is very high confidence that stays high even as accuracy falls. Roger Brown and James Kulik coined the term flashbulb memory in 1977 for vivid, detailed memories of the circumstances in which a person heard shocking news, their main example being the assassination of John F. Kennedy in 1963. People recall where they were, what they were doing, who told them, how they felt, how others felt and what happened next (the canonical categories). Brown and Kulik thought a special 'Now Print!' mechanism, a term from Robert Livingston (1967), fixed these memories permanently. Later studies tested that by recording memories right after an event and re-testing later. Ulric Neisser and Nicole Harsch (1992) had students write down, the morning after, how they heard of the Challenger explosion (28 January 1986) and asked again about two and a half years later: accuracy was poor, about a quarter were wrong on every major detail, yet confidence stayed high, and some insisted the new version was right even when shown their own handwritten account. Jennifer Talarico and David Rubin (2003) recorded students' 9/11 memories on 12 September 2001 alongside an everyday memory. Over the following months both kinds lost consistency at the same rate, but only the flashbulb memories kept their vividness and the person's belief in their accuracy. Their conclusion is the exam line: flashbulb memories are special not in their accuracy but in the confidence attached to them. Emotional arousal at encoding (the amygdala) and endless retelling through media and conversation explain the vividness.", "topic": "Q39 · Flashbulb memory: confidence stays high"}, {"q": "An intervention in which the person deliberately does, or wishes for, the very opposite of the therapeutic goal in order to reach that goal is:", "options": {"a": "Paradoxical intention", "b": "Dereflection", "c": "Negative practice", "d": "Reciprocal inhibition"}, "correct": "a", "section": "recall", "explanation": "The answer is paradoxical intention, a technique of Viktor Frankl, the Viennese psychiatrist who founded logotherapy. He first described it in 1939, and Man's Search for Meaning is the popular account of his ideas. Frankl noticed a loop he called anticipatory anxiety: a person fears a symptom (blushing, sweating, not falling asleep), the fear makes the symptom more likely, and its appearance strengthens the fear. Paradoxical intention breaks the loop by asking the person to intend, or even wish for, the very thing he fears, ideally with humour. The insomniac is told to try to stay awake as long as he can; the man afraid of sweating in public is told to show everyone how much he can really sweat. The moment you try to produce the symptom, the fear of it has nothing to work on, and the symptom usually fades. Frankl rooted this in self-detachment, the human capacity to step back from oneself and even laugh at oneself. He used it for phobias, obsessive fears and sleep problems, and behaviour therapists later tested it in trials, for example for sleep-onset insomnia (L. Michael Ascher and Ralph Turner, 1979). The family-therapy cousin is symptom prescription, used by Milton Erickson, Jay Haley and the Palo Alto group (Paul Watzlawick and colleagues, 1974), where the therapist instructs the family to keep doing the problem behaviour. The stem's cue, doing the opposite of the goal in order to reach the goal, is the textbook definition of paradoxical intention.", "topic": "Q40 · Paradoxical intention (Frankl)"}, {"q": "In ICD-11, gender incongruence is placed in:", "options": {"a": "Chapter 6, Mental, behavioural or neurodevelopmental disorders", "b": "Chapter 5, Endocrine, nutritional or metabolic diseases", "c": "Chapter 17, Conditions related to sexual health", "d": "Nowhere: it was removed from ICD-11 altogether"}, "correct": "c", "section": "recall", "explanation": "The answer is Chapter 17, Conditions related to sexual health. ICD-11 was adopted by the World Health Assembly in May 2019 and came into effect on 1 January 2022. In ICD-10 the topic sat in Chapter V (mental and behavioural disorders) as F64, gender identity disorders, which included transsexualism (F64.0), dual-role transvestism (F64.1) and gender identity disorder of childhood (F64.2). ICD-11 renamed it gender incongruence and moved it out of the mental disorders chapter into a new Chapter 17, Conditions related to sexual health. WHO's reasoning: being transgender is not a mental illness and filing it under mental disorders added stigma, but keeping a code keeps access to gender-affirming health care, which health systems and insurers usually pay for only when there is a code. ICD-11 has two codes. HA60, gender incongruence of adolescence or adulthood: a marked and persistent incongruence between experienced gender and assigned sex, often with a wish to transition, present for several months. HA61, gender incongruence of childhood: in children before puberty, persisting for about two years. Gender-variant behaviour and preferences alone are not enough for either. Sexual dysfunctions and sexual pain disorders moved to Chapter 17 too, while paraphilic disorders stayed in Chapter 6. DSM-5 (2013) and DSM-5-TR (2022) do not follow ICD here: they keep the condition in the manual as gender dysphoria, where the diagnosis rests on clinically significant distress or impairment.", "topic": "Q41 · ICD-11: gender incongruence moved to Chapter 17"}, {"q": "Bavisha lost her job. Since then she is stressed, has stopped eating properly and cannot get herself to do anything. According to yogic philosophy, the main issue she is dealing with is:", "options": {"a": "Yama alone", "b": "Tamas alone", "c": "Both yama and tamas", "d": "Rajas alone"}, "correct": "b", "section": "recall", "explanation": "The answer is tamas alone. The theory of the three gunas (triguna) comes from Samkhya philosophy, traditionally traced to the sage Kapila and set out in Ishvarakrishna's Samkhya Karika, and it runs through the Bhagavad Gita (chapters 14, 17 and 18). Prakriti, the material nature that includes the mind, is made of three gunas (strands or qualities). All three are always present in different proportions, and whichever dominates colours a person's thinking, feeling and behaviour. Sattva is light, clarity, calm, knowledge and contentment. Rajas is activity, passion, desire, restlessness, attachment, anger and greed. Tamas is darkness, heaviness, inertia, dullness, delusion, excess sleep and the inability to act. Bavisha after losing her job is not driven, angry or racing about, which would be rajas; she is stuck, withdrawn, not eating and not functioning, which is tamas dominating. In Indian psychology writing a tamas-heavy state is the closest match to depression (low energy, slowing, withdrawal), rajas to anxious, agitated or manic states, and sattva to mental health. Treatment in this frame means reducing tamas and building sattva: routine, activity, sattvic food, yoga and meditation. One research measure is the Vedic Personality Inventory by David Wolf (1998). Yama, the other option word, is not a guna or a mental state at all; it is the first of Patanjali's eight limbs of yoga, the five ethical restraints. Your recall had 'Yama alone' and 'Tamas alone' as options, so the other two here are rebuilt.", "topic": "Yoga philosophy · Bavisha: tamas"}, {"q": "Which of the eight limbs of yoga is concentration, directing the mind's energy to a single point?", "options": {"a": "Pratyahara", "b": "Jnana", "c": "Dhyana", "d": "Dharana"}, "correct": "d", "section": "recall", "explanation": "The answer is dharana. Patanjali's Yoga Sutras (compiled somewhere between about 200 BCE and 400 CE) define yoga in sutra 1.2 as 'yogas chitta vritti nirodhah', the stilling of the fluctuations of the mind, and lay out an eightfold path, ashtanga yoga, in sutra 2.29: (1) yama, ethical restraints; (2) niyama, personal observances; (3) asana, posture; (4) pranayama, breath regulation; (5) pratyahara, withdrawal of the senses from their objects; (6) dharana, concentration; (7) dhyana, meditation; (8) samadhi, absorption. Sutra 3.1 defines dharana as binding the mind to one place (desha bandhah chittasya): holding attention on a single point or object, such as the breath, a spot between the eyebrows or a candle flame. That is exactly 'concentrating' and 'directing mental energy to one point in the mind', the two wordings in your paper. Dhyana (3.2) is what dharana becomes when the focus flows unbroken, like oil poured from one vessel to another, without effort. Samadhi (3.3) is when only the object shines and the sense of a separate meditator drops away. Dharana, dhyana and samadhi practised together on one object are called samyama (3.4); these three are the inner limbs (antaranga), and the first five are the outer limbs (bahiranga). Fast ladder: pratyahara pulls the senses in, dharana points the mind, dhyana keeps it pointed, samadhi merges with the point. Your recall had pratyahara and jnana as options; the fourth is rebuilt.", "topic": "Yoga philosophy · Dharana is concentration"}, {"q": "Kegel exercises strengthen which part of the body?", "options": {"a": "The abdominal (core) muscles", "b": "The pelvic floor muscles", "c": "The lower back (lumbar) muscles", "d": "The gluteal (buttock) muscles"}, "correct": "b", "section": "recall", "explanation": "The answer is the pelvic floor muscles. Arnold Kegel, an American gynaecologist, described the exercises in 1948 as a non-surgical treatment for women with urinary incontinence after childbirth. The pelvic floor is the hammock of muscle across the bottom of the pelvis, mainly the levator ani group, whose pubococcygeus part (the PC muscle) runs from the pubic bone to the tailbone. It supports the bladder, bowel and uterus and controls the urethral and anal openings. A Kegel is a squeeze-and-lift of these muscles (the ones used to stop urine midstream, though doing that routinely is not advised), held for a few seconds and then relaxed, repeated in sets through the day. Kegel used a pressure gauge placed in the vagina, the perineometer, so the patient could see her own squeeze, which makes his method one of the earliest forms of biofeedback. Uses today: stress urinary incontinence (leaking on coughing, sneezing or lifting), recovery after childbirth, pelvic organ prolapse, incontinence in men after prostate surgery, and faecal incontinence. The psychology link, and the reason it turns up in entrance papers, is twofold. In health psychology and behavioural medicine, biofeedback-assisted pelvic floor training is a standard behavioural treatment. In sex therapy, pelvic floor exercises are part of the directed masturbation programme for female orgasmic disorder (Joseph LoPiccolo and W. Charles Lobitz, 1972) and are used as an add-on for premature ejaculation, while pelvic floor relaxation work with graded dilators is used for vaginismus.", "topic": "Kegel exercises: the pelvic floor"}, {"q": "In George Vaillant's hierarchy, which one of these is a mature defence?", "options": {"a": "Repression", "b": "Projection", "c": "Reaction formation", "d": "Suppression"}, "correct": "d", "section": "cluster", "explanation": "The answer is suppression, the only one of the four that Vaillant (1977) placed at Level IV, the mature level, with altruism, humour, sublimation and anticipation. Suppression is a conscious or semi-conscious decision to postpone attention to a worry: 'I will deal with the result after the exam.' The feeling is not denied or distorted; it is parked and picked up later. That is why it is called adaptive: the person stays aware of reality and keeps functioning. The near-identical word repression is the trap. Repression, Freud's original and central defence, is unconscious: the threatening thought or memory is kept out of awareness without the person choosing it, and it tends to return in disguised forms such as slips, symptoms and dreams. Vaillant put repression at Level III, neurotic. A useful pairing: suppression is conscious and mature, repression is unconscious and neurotic. The other mature defences are altruism (getting satisfaction from helping others with the very thing that troubles you), humour (seeing the funny side of a painful situation without hurting anyone), sublimation (channelling an unacceptable impulse into a valued activity, as in turning aggression into competitive sport) and anticipation (realistic planning for future discomfort). In the Grant Study, Harvard men followed from the late 1930s, mature defences at midlife predicted better physical health, income, marriage and enjoyment of life decades later. Exam stems often describe a person and ask for the defence: 'decides not to think about the bill until payday' is suppression; 'has no memory of the accident' is repression.", "topic": "Vaillant · suppression is mature, repression is neurotic"}, {"q": "Which group contains only immature defences in Vaillant's (1977) scheme?", "options": {"a": "Projection, acting out, passive aggression, hypochondriasis", "b": "Repression, displacement, reaction formation, intellectualization", "c": "Altruism, humour, sublimation, anticipation", "d": "Delusional projection, psychotic denial, distortion"}, "correct": "a", "section": "cluster", "explanation": "The answer is projection, acting out, passive aggression and hypochondriasis, four of the five Level II immature defences (the fifth is schizoid fantasy). Vaillant called them immature because they are normal in adolescence but, when they persist in adults, they distort how others are seen and damage relationships; they are the defences most often seen in personality disorders and substance misuse. Projection: attributing your own unacceptable feelings to someone else ('he hates me' when you resent him). Acting out: discharging an impulse directly in action without reflecting on the feeling behind it (getting drunk and picking a fight after a rejection). Passive aggression: expressing hostility indirectly, through procrastination, sulking, 'forgetting' or failing on purpose. Hypochondriasis: turning reproach toward others into complaints of physical illness. Schizoid fantasy: retreating into an inner fantasy world to avoid intimacy. The other three options are the other three levels as complete blocks. Repression, displacement, reaction formation and intellectualization are Level III, neurotic, the level where dissociation also sits. Altruism, humour, sublimation and anticipation are Level IV, mature, together with suppression. Delusional projection, psychotic denial and distortion are Level I, psychotic. Learn each level as a block, because papers either name one defence and ask its level, as your NIMHANS paper did with dissociation, or list four and ask which one does not belong. One trap to watch: projection is immature, but delusional projection, where the projected belief becomes a fixed delusion, is psychotic.", "topic": "Vaillant · the immature level as a block"}, {"q": "In systematic desensitization, the 0 to 100 scale on which the client rates how anxious each scene in the hierarchy makes him is the:", "options": {"a": "Fear Survey Schedule", "b": "State-Trait Anxiety Inventory (STAI)", "c": "Subjective Units of Disturbance Scale (SUDS)", "d": "Hamilton Anxiety Rating Scale (HAM-A)"}, "correct": "c", "section": "cluster", "explanation": "The answer is SUDS, the Subjective Units of Disturbance (often called Distress) Scale, introduced by Joseph Wolpe in The Practice of Behavior Therapy (1969). The client rates his own anxiety from 0 (completely calm) to 100 (the worst anxiety he can imagine). SUDS is used twice in desensitization. First, to build the hierarchy: each feared situation gets a SUDS rating and the scenes are ordered with fairly even steps between them, often 5 to 10 points, so no jump is too big. Second, during treatment: after the client imagines a scene while relaxed he reports his SUDS, and the therapist moves up the hierarchy only when the current scene has come down close to zero. SUDS is a quick idiographic measure, made for one person in the moment, not a standardised test with norms. It is now used across exposure therapies, in EMDR (Francine Shapiro) and in exposure and response prevention for OCD. A typical hierarchy for a fear of injections starts around 10 with reading the word 'injection', runs through about 40 for sitting in a clinic waiting room and about 70 for watching someone else get an injection, and ends at 100 with getting one yourself. Two other Wolpe items worth knowing: the Fear Survey Schedule (with Peter Lang, 1964), used at the start to map what the client fears, and assertiveness training, which he also used as an anxiety-inhibiting response. If a stem says 'subjective', '0 to 100' and 'hierarchy' together, it is SUDS.", "topic": "Systematic desensitization · SUDS"}, {"q": "Flooding differs from systematic desensitization mainly because flooding:", "options": {"a": "Pairs the feared stimulus with an unpleasant stimulus such as a shock", "b": "Pairs the feared stimulus with relaxation, one step at a time", "c": "Can only be done in imagination", "d": "Exposes the person to the most feared stimulus at full intensity, with no hierarchy and no relaxation, until the anxiety dies down"}, "correct": "d", "section": "cluster", "explanation": "The answer is full-intensity exposure with no hierarchy and no relaxation. Both techniques rest on conditioning, but on different mechanisms. Systematic desensitization (Joseph Wolpe, 1958) is counterconditioning: anxiety is replaced by an incompatible response, relaxation, one rung of the hierarchy at a time. Flooding is extinction: the person is kept in front of the most feared stimulus, in real life or in imagination, and prevented from escaping until the anxiety peaks and falls on its own. Escape is what keeps a phobia alive. O. Hobart Mowrer's two-factor theory (1947) explains why: fear is learned by classical conditioning and maintained by operant avoidance, because every escape is negatively reinforced by relief, so the person never stays long enough to learn that the stimulus is harmless. Block the escape and that learning finally happens. Implosive therapy (Thomas Stampfl and Donald Levis, 1967) is flooding in imagination, with scenes exaggerated and laced with psychodynamic themes. Flooding is faster but harder to tolerate and has more drop-outs; graded exposure sits in between and is what most CBT uses today. Aversion therapy is a different family altogether: it pairs an unwanted behaviour with something unpleasant to create an aversion rather than remove a fear. Reading the stem: 'maximum intensity', 'no escape' or 'prolonged exposure until anxiety subsides' is flooding; 'gradual', 'hierarchy' or 'relaxation' is desensitization; 'pairing with nausea or shock' is aversion therapy.", "topic": "Flooding vs systematic desensitization"}, {"q": "In habit reversal training, a good competing response must be:", "options": {"a": "An unpleasant consequence given each time the habit occurs", "b": "Physically incompatible with the habit, inconspicuous, and held for about a minute or until the urge passes", "c": "The habit itself, performed deliberately and repeatedly until fatigue sets in", "d": "A relaxation exercise practised once a day"}, "correct": "b", "section": "cluster", "explanation": "The answer is a response that is physically incompatible with the habit, socially inconspicuous and held for about a minute or until the urge passes. In Nathan Azrin and Gregory Nunn's habit reversal (1973), the competing response is the active ingredient that follows awareness training. Once the client notices the habit or the urge, he does something that makes the habit impossible at that moment, usually by tensing the opposing muscles. Examples: for hair pulling or nail biting, clench the fists or hold an object; for a head-jerk tic, gently tense the neck muscles while keeping the head still; for an eye-blink tic, slow deliberate blinking; for a vocal tic, slow rhythmic breathing through the nose. It must be inconspicuous so it can be used in class or on a bus without embarrassment, and it is practised until it becomes the automatic reaction to the urge. Azrin and Nunn had clients hold it for three minutes; later versions use about one minute. Why it works is still debated: it physically blocks the habit, interrupts the habit chain, and lets the urge fade without being rewarded. For tics, the urge is a premonitory sensation that the tic relieves, so each tic is negatively reinforced; riding out the urge with a competing response breaks that cycle. Social support, a parent or partner praising use of the competing response and prompting it, completes the simplified version of HRT (Miltenberger, Fuqua and Woods, 1998).", "topic": "HRT · the competing response"}, {"q": "Habit reversal training is a first-line behavioural treatment for all of the following EXCEPT:", "options": {"a": "Trichotillomania (hair-pulling disorder)", "b": "Chronic tic disorder and Tourette's disorder", "c": "Obsessive-compulsive disorder", "d": "Excoriation (skin-picking) disorder"}, "correct": "c", "section": "cluster", "explanation": "The answer is obsessive-compulsive disorder, whose first-line psychological treatment is exposure and response prevention (ERP), which grew out of Victor Meyer's work in 1966 and was developed further by Edna Foa, usually alongside SSRIs. HRT targets a different family: tics and body-focused repetitive behaviours (BFRBs), the habits aimed at one's own body such as hair pulling (trichotillomania), skin picking (excoriation disorder), nail biting and cheek chewing. The trap is the DSM-5 (2013) chapter layout: trichotillomania and excoriation disorder sit in the obsessive-compulsive and related disorders chapter, right next to OCD, so it is easy to assume they share a treatment. They do not. In OCD the compulsion is driven by an obsession (a thought such as 'my hands are contaminated') and is done to neutralise the anxiety that thought creates. In BFRBs and tics there is usually no obsession behind the act, just an urge, tension or sensation that the habit relieves, often with little awareness. That is why HRT starts with awareness training and a competing response, while ERP exposes the person to the obsession's trigger and blocks the ritual. For tics, the Comprehensive Behavioral Intervention for Tics (CBIT; John Piacentini and colleagues, 2010, a randomised trial in children published in JAMA) is HRT plus function-based work and is recommended first-line. On an EXCEPT stem, three options are HRT targets and you are hunting for the outlier, so read every option before marking.", "topic": "HRT · targets (EXCEPT stem)"}, {"q": "Talarico and Rubin (2003) compared students' 9/11 memories with their everyday memories. The flashbulb memories differed in:", "options": {"a": "Vividness and belief in their accuracy, not in consistency", "b": "Consistency: they changed far less over time", "c": "Accuracy: they were near perfect after eight months", "d": "Amount of detail: they were forgotten faster"}, "correct": "a", "section": "cluster", "explanation": "The answer is vividness and belief in their accuracy, not consistency. Jennifer Talarico and David Rubin at Duke University had 54 students record, on 12 September 2001, how they first heard about the attacks, along with a recent everyday memory (a party, a study session) as a control. Each student was tested again either 1, 6 or 32 weeks later. Both kinds of memory became less consistent at the same rate: details changed, dropped out or were added. What differed was the experience of remembering. For the flashbulb memories, ratings of vividness, of reliving the event and of belief in the memory's accuracy stayed high, while for everyday memories they fell. The paper's title says it all: 'Confidence, not consistency, characterizes flashbulb memories'. This fits Ulric Neisser and Nicole Harsch's Challenger study (1992) and goes against Roger Brown and James Kulik's (1977) idea of a special 'Now Print!' mechanism that would preserve these memories exactly. The modern view is that flashbulb memories are ordinary memories made to feel special by strong emotion at encoding and by heavy rehearsal, since the event is replayed in the media and retold again and again, and every retelling can reshape the memory. A large national 9/11 survey led by William Hirst (2009, with a ten-year follow-up in 2015) found the same pattern: consistency dropped in the first year and then levelled off, while confidence stayed high throughout. One-line version for the exam: high confidence, falling accuracy.", "topic": "Flashbulb memory · Talarico and Rubin (2003)"}, {"q": "Current research explains the vividness of flashbulb memories mainly by:", "options": {"a": "A special 'Now Print!' mechanism that stores the scene permanently and exactly", "b": "The absence of any interference from later events", "c": "Photographic (eidetic) memory, which only some people have", "d": "Emotional arousal at encoding plus repeated rehearsal and retelling"}, "correct": "d", "section": "cluster", "explanation": "The answer is emotional arousal at encoding plus repeated rehearsal. When Roger Brown and James Kulik named flashbulb memories in 1977 they proposed a special neural 'Now Print!' mechanism, borrowed from Robert Livingston (1967), that switched on for surprising, consequential events and fixed the whole scene like a photograph. The diary studies that followed (Neisser and Harsch, 1992; Talarico and Rubin, 2003) showed these memories drift like any other, so no special mechanism is needed. Two ordinary processes explain the vividness. The first is emotion at encoding: arousal releases adrenaline and cortisol, the amygdala signals the hippocampus, and consolidation of the central emotional details is strengthened (James McGaugh's work on emotional memory). Arousal also narrows attention to the central event, the same narrowing behind Elizabeth Loftus's weapon focus, so peripheral details such as who told you are encoded weakly, and those are exactly the details flashbulb studies find inconsistent. The second is rehearsal: such events are replayed on television and talked about for weeks, and each retelling rebuilds the memory, sometimes mixing in things learned later. Kathy Pezdek (2003) found that most students she surveyed said they had seen the first plane hit the World Trade Center on live television on 11 September, although that footage was not broadcast until the next day. Neisser called such memories benchmarks: we keep them vivid because they tie our own life story to history. Exam pairing: Brown and Kulik 1977, special mechanism and high confidence; Neisser and Harsch 1992 and Talarico and Rubin 2003, accuracy falls while confidence does not.", "topic": "Flashbulb memory · why it feels so vivid"}, {"q": "A man with sleep-onset insomnia is told to lie in bed with the lights off and try as hard as he can to stay awake. This technique is:", "options": {"a": "Paradoxical intention", "b": "Stimulus control", "c": "Sleep restriction", "d": "Dereflection"}, "correct": "a", "section": "cluster", "explanation": "The answer is paradoxical intention, Viktor Frankl's technique applied to sleep. Sleep-onset insomnia is often kept going by performance anxiety about sleep: the harder a person tries to fall asleep, the more aroused he becomes, because sleep is one of the few things effort cannot produce. Frankl's anticipatory-anxiety loop runs here: fear of another sleepless night produces arousal, the arousal produces the sleepless night, and that strengthens the fear. Telling him to try to stay awake removes the effort to sleep, so the performance anxiety has nothing to feed on, and sleep tends to come. Behaviour therapists tested it in controlled trials, for example L. Michael Ascher and Ralph Turner (1979), and it is one of the recognised psychological treatments for insomnia, usually as a component within cognitive behavioural therapy for insomnia (CBT-I), whose core parts are stimulus control, sleep restriction, sleep hygiene, relaxation and cognitive therapy. Tell the CBT-I parts apart by the instruction given. Stimulus control (Richard Bootzin, 1972): use the bed only for sleep and sex, go to bed only when sleepy, get up and leave the room if not asleep in about 20 minutes, keep a fixed rising time, no naps. Sleep restriction (Arthur Spielman, 1987): limit time in bed to the time actually spent asleep to build sleep pressure, then extend it gradually. Sleep hygiene: caffeine, alcohol, exercise timing, a dark and quiet room. Paradoxical intention: stay in bed and try to remain awake. The stem gives the last instruction.", "topic": "Paradoxical intention · insomnia"}, {"q": "Frankl's technique for 'hyper-reflection', in which a person stops watching himself and turns his attention outward, for example from his own sexual performance to his partner, is:", "options": {"a": "Paradoxical intention", "b": "Sensate focus", "c": "Dereflection", "d": "Socratic dialogue"}, "correct": "c", "section": "cluster", "explanation": "The answer is dereflection. Viktor Frankl described two faulty patterns that keep symptoms going. Hyper-intention is trying too hard for something that can only come as a by-product, like sleep, orgasm or happiness. Hyper-reflection is excessive attention to oneself: watching one's own body, feelings or performance. His two techniques match them. Paradoxical intention handles anticipatory anxiety, and dereflection handles hyper-reflection. In dereflection the person is helped to ignore the symptom and redirect attention outward, to another person, a task or a meaning, so that self-observation stops interfering. Frankl's classic examples were sexual: a man whose erection fails because he is monitoring it, or a woman who cannot reach orgasm because she is watching for it, is told to stop striving and give attention to the partner. Logotherapy, Frankl's school, is often called the third Viennese school of psychotherapy, after Sigmund Freud's psychoanalysis and Alfred Adler's individual psychology. It holds that the primary human motive is the will to meaning (against Freud's will to pleasure and Adler's will to power), and that a lack of meaning produces an existential vacuum and what Frankl called noogenic neurosis. Its methods are paradoxical intention, dereflection and Socratic dialogue to help the client discover meaning. His own years in Nazi concentration camps are told in Man's Search for Meaning. Exam rule: fear of a symptom, paradoxical intention; excessive self-watching, dereflection.", "topic": "Frankl · dereflection"}, {"q": "When gender incongruence moved out of the mental disorders chapter in ICD-11, which of these stayed in Chapter 6 (Mental, behavioural or neurodevelopmental disorders)?", "options": {"a": "Sexual dysfunctions", "b": "Paraphilic disorders", "c": "Sexual pain disorders", "d": "Gender incongruence of childhood"}, "correct": "b", "section": "cluster", "explanation": "The answer is paraphilic disorders. ICD-11 created Chapter 17, Conditions related to sexual health, and moved three groups into it from the old mental and behavioural chapter: gender incongruence (HA60 for adolescence or adulthood, HA61 for childhood), sexual dysfunctions (HA00 to HA03: hypoactive sexual desire dysfunction, sexual arousal dysfunctions, orgasmic dysfunctions and ejaculatory dysfunctions) and sexual pain disorders (sexual pain-penetration disorder, HA20, which replaces vaginismus and dyspareunia). WHO's reasoning: these conditions are not best understood as mental disorders, and ICD-10 had split them on an outdated mind-body line, with non-organic sexual dysfunctions in the mental chapter and organic ones elsewhere. ICD-11 puts them in one place and lets the clinician add aetiological qualifiers instead (associated with a medical condition, with psychological or behavioural factors, with a substance or medication, with lack of knowledge, and so on). Paraphilic disorders stayed in Chapter 6 (6D30 to 6D3Z: exhibitionistic, voyeuristic, pedophilic, coercive sexual sadism and frotteuristic disorders, plus other paraphilic disorders) because they centre on acting on, or being markedly distressed by, sexual arousal involving people who do not or cannot consent. ICD-11 also dropped several old categories as not disorders, such as fetishism, fetishistic transvestism and sadomasochism between consenting adults. Chapter numbers worth holding: 6 is mental disorders, 7 is sleep-wake disorders (which also moved out of the mental chapter), 17 is sexual health.", "topic": "ICD-11 · what stayed in the mental chapter"}, {"q": "The DSM-5 (2013) diagnosis that corresponds to ICD-11 gender incongruence is:", "options": {"a": "Gender identity disorder", "b": "Transsexualism", "c": "Gender incongruence", "d": "Gender dysphoria"}, "correct": "d", "section": "cluster", "explanation": "The answer is gender dysphoria. The label has changed with almost every edition, and each change shows the thinking of its time. DSM-III (1980) introduced transsexualism (for adults) and gender identity disorder of childhood. DSM-IV (1994) merged them into gender identity disorder, which framed the identity itself as the disorder. DSM-5 (2013) renamed it gender dysphoria and moved the focus to distress: the diagnosis is about the clinically significant distress or impairment that comes from the incongruence between one's experienced or expressed gender and assigned gender, not about being transgender as such. DSM-5 criteria: a marked incongruence lasting at least 6 months. Adolescents and adults need at least 2 of 6 indicators, such as a strong desire to be rid of one's sex characteristics or to be treated as another gender. Children need at least 6 of 8, one of which must be a strong desire to be of another gender or an insistence that one is. Gender dysphoria has its own chapter in DSM-5, separate from sexual dysfunctions and paraphilic disorders. DSM-5-TR (2022) kept the diagnosis and updated the wording, for example 'experienced gender' instead of 'desired gender' and 'gender-affirming medical procedure' instead of 'cross-sex medical procedure'. ICD-11 went further: it calls the condition gender incongruence, requires no distress for the diagnosis, and placed it outside the mental disorders chapter. So the DSM keeps it as a mental disorder defined by distress, and ICD-11 keeps a code outside mental disorders for access to care.", "topic": "DSM-5 · gender dysphoria"}, {"q": "In the triguna theory, the guna marked by restlessness, passion, desire and over-activity is:", "options": {"a": "Sattva", "b": "Rajas", "c": "Tamas", "d": "Ahamkara"}, "correct": "b", "section": "cluster", "explanation": "The answer is rajas. The three gunas are best learned as a table of opposites, because exam stems describe a person and ask which guna dominates. Sattva: light, clear, calm, balanced, truthful, content, wise; the sattvic person is self-controlled and helpful, and sattva is linked with mental health and wisdom. Rajas: active, passionate, ambitious, restless, craving, attached; under stress it turns into anger, greed, anxiety, irritability and the endless chase of pleasure, success or status. Tamas: dark, heavy, dull, inert, deluded, sleepy, negligent; the tamasic person is lazy, confused and stuck. In one line: rajas is energy that moves, tamas is energy that is blocked, sattva is energy in balance. The Bhagavad Gita (chapter 14) says sattva binds a person through attachment to happiness and knowledge, rajas through attachment to action, and tamas through negligence, laziness and sleep; chapter 17 extends the scheme to food, worship and giving (sattvic food is fresh and light, rajasic food spicy and stimulating, tamasic food stale or heavy). All three are present in everyone; personality is the balance, and the balance shifts with lifestyle and practice. The clinical mapping used in Indian psychology: excess rajas looks like anxiety, agitation, anger or mania; excess tamas looks like depression, apathy and lethargy. Quick translation for scenario stems: hyper and craving is rajas, stuck and dull is tamas, calm and clear is sattva. Your Bavisha question was the tamas version of this.", "topic": "Triguna · rajas"}, {"q": "The concept of the three gunas comes mainly from which school of Indian philosophy, alongside the Bhagavad Gita?", "options": {"a": "Nyaya", "b": "Charvaka", "c": "Samkhya", "d": "Mimamsa"}, "correct": "c", "section": "cluster", "explanation": "The answer is Samkhya, one of the six orthodox (astika) schools of Indian philosophy, traditionally founded by the sage Kapila; its oldest surviving text is Ishvarakrishna's Samkhya Karika (around the 4th to 5th century CE). Samkhya is a dualism of two eternal realities. Purusha is pure consciousness: the witness, unchanging and inactive. Prakriti is primal matter, which includes not only the body and the world but also the mind. Prakriti is made of the three gunas, sattva, rajas and tamas. When they are in perfect balance nothing manifests; when the balance is disturbed in the presence of purusha, prakriti unfolds into the world in a fixed order: buddhi (intellect) first, then ahamkara (ego), then manas (mind) with the five senses, the five organs of action, the five subtle elements and the five gross elements, 23 products in all, or 25 principles (tattvas) counting purusha and prakriti. Suffering comes from purusha wrongly identifying with the workings of prakriti, and liberation (kaivalya) is the discriminating knowledge that separates the two. Yoga is Samkhya's partner school: Patanjali takes over Samkhya's metaphysics, adds Ishvara (a special purusha) and a practical method, the eight limbs. The six orthodox schools are usually paired: Samkhya with Yoga, Nyaya with Vaisheshika, Mimamsa with Vedanta. Psychology papers use Samkhya for the gunas, for purusha and prakriti, and for the antahkarana (buddhi, ahamkara and manas).", "topic": "Triguna · Samkhya is the source"}, {"q": "During practice, a student learns to stop her attention being pulled by the sounds, smells and sights around her and to turn her senses inward. Which limb of yoga is this?", "options": {"a": "Pratyahara", "b": "Dharana", "c": "Pranayama", "d": "Niyama"}, "correct": "a", "section": "cluster", "explanation": "The answer is pratyahara, the fifth of Patanjali's eight limbs. The word means drawing back (prati, against, and ahara, food or what is taken in): the senses stop feeding on their objects and are drawn inward, the way a tortoise pulls in its limbs, which is the Bhagavad Gita's image (2.58). Patanjali's sutra 2.54 describes it as the senses withdrawing from their objects and following the mind inward. Pratyahara is the hinge of ashtanga yoga. The first four limbs (yama, niyama, asana, pranayama) work on conduct, body and breath; the last three (dharana, dhyana, samadhi) work on the mind; pratyahara is the bridge, since a mind still pulled by every noise cannot concentrate. So the sequence a stem may walk you through is: first switch off the outside (pratyahara), then fix on one point (dharana), then hold it in an unbroken flow (dhyana), then absorption (samadhi). This is the exact pair that was set against each other in your paper: 'withdrawal of the senses' is pratyahara, 'concentration on one point' or 'directing mental energy to one point' is dharana. Lock it as senses in, pratyahara; mind fixed, dharana. The other options in this question are earlier limbs: pranayama is breath regulation (the fourth limb), and niyama is the five observances (the second limb: shaucha or cleanliness, santosha or contentment, tapas or austerity, svadhyaya or self-study, ishvara pranidhana or surrender to the Lord).", "topic": "Eight limbs · pratyahara vs dharana"}, {"q": "Which is the correct order of the last four of Patanjali's eight limbs?", "options": {"a": "Dharana, pratyahara, dhyana, samadhi", "b": "Pratyahara, dharana, dhyana, samadhi", "c": "Pratyahara, dhyana, dharana, samadhi", "d": "Dhyana, dharana, pratyahara, samadhi"}, "correct": "b", "section": "cluster", "explanation": "The answer is pratyahara, dharana, dhyana, samadhi. Patanjali's full list (Yoga Sutra 2.29) is yama, niyama, asana, pranayama, pratyahara, dharana, dhyana, samadhi. A line to hold it: Yogis Never Argue, Peaceful People Don't Disturb Silence. The order is a progression from outer to inner, and each limb prepares the next. Yama (the five restraints: ahimsa or non-violence, satya or truthfulness, asteya or non-stealing, brahmacharya or sexual restraint, aparigraha or non-possessiveness) and niyama (the five observances: shaucha, santosha, tapas, svadhyaya and ishvara pranidhana) settle conduct. Asana steadies the body; sutra 2.46 says posture should be steady and comfortable (sthira sukham asanam). Pranayama regulates the breath. Pratyahara withdraws the senses. Then come the three inner limbs: dharana fixes the mind on one point (3.1), dhyana is that focus flowing unbroken (3.2), and samadhi is absorption in which only the object remains (3.3). Practised together on one object, the three are called samyama (3.4). The first five are the outer limbs (bahiranga), the last three the inner limbs (antaranga). The pairs that get swapped are the ones that sound alike. Pranayama and pratyahara both start with 'pra': breath comes before the senses. Dharana and dhyana both start with 'dh': fixing comes before flowing. If you can place those two pairs, the order question is free.", "topic": "Eight limbs · the order"}, {"q": "Kegel (pelvic floor) exercises are a standard first-line treatment for:", "options": {"a": "Stress urinary incontinence after childbirth", "b": "Nocturnal enuresis (bed-wetting) in a 7-year-old", "c": "Chronic tic disorder", "d": "Sleep-onset insomnia"}, "correct": "a", "section": "cluster", "explanation": "The answer is stress urinary incontinence after childbirth, the condition Arnold Kegel designed the exercises for in 1948. Stress incontinence is leakage when pressure in the abdomen rises (coughing, sneezing, laughing, lifting) because the pelvic floor and the urethral sphincter are too weak to hold; pregnancy and vaginal delivery stretch and sometimes injure these muscles. Pelvic floor muscle training is the recommended first-line treatment, ahead of surgery, usually for at least three months, and works better when supervised or helped by biofeedback than when learned from a leaflet. It also helps urge incontinence (together with bladder training), the symptoms of pelvic organ prolapse, incontinence in men after prostate surgery, and faecal incontinence. The trap in the options is enuresis. Bed-wetting in a child (enuresis, diagnosable from age 5 in DSM-5) is not a muscle-strength problem. The first-line behavioural treatment is the enuresis alarm, the bell-and-pad of O. Hobart Mowrer and Willie Mae Mowrer (1938): urine completes a circuit, the bell wakes the child, and over weeks a full bladder becomes the cue to wake or hold on, which is classical conditioning with an avoidance component. Desmopressin is the drug option. Dry-bed training (Nathan Azrin and colleagues, 1974) adds scheduled waking, cleanliness training and positive practice to the alarm. So: adult leakage on coughing, Kegels; a child wetting the bed, bell-and-pad.", "topic": "Kegels · what they treat (vs bell-and-pad)"}, {"q": "Arnold Kegel (1948) taught his exercises with a perineometer, a pressure gauge that let the patient watch her own muscle squeeze. This made his method an early form of:", "options": {"a": "Aversion therapy", "b": "Token economy", "c": "Systematic desensitization", "d": "Biofeedback"}, "correct": "d", "section": "cluster", "explanation": "The answer is biofeedback: using an instrument to make a bodily process the person cannot normally sense visible or audible, so he can learn to control it. Kegel's perineometer was an air-filled chamber placed in the vagina and connected to a pressure dial. The patient watched the needle rise as she contracted the right muscles, which taught her to find a muscle group most people cannot locate by feel, and let her track her strength as it grew. Biofeedback as a named field came later, in the late 1960s, after Neal Miller's experiments suggested that autonomic responses could be operantly conditioned; the Biofeedback Research Society was founded in 1969. The learning principle is operant: the feedback signal is both information and reinforcement for each small change in the right direction. The main types in clinical psychology: EMG biofeedback for muscle tension (tension headache, pelvic floor training, rehabilitation after stroke); thermal or skin-temperature biofeedback (hand warming for migraine and Raynaud's disease); electrodermal or GSR biofeedback for arousal and anxiety; heart rate variability biofeedback; and EEG biofeedback, called neurofeedback (ADHD, epilepsy). Pelvic floor biofeedback today uses surface EMG or pressure probes and is still a standard part of treating incontinence and some sexual pain problems. Exam pairing: feedback of a body signal is biofeedback; pairing with something unpleasant is aversion therapy; pairing with relaxation is systematic desensitization; tokens exchanged for rewards is a token economy.", "topic": "Kegels · the perineometer was early biofeedback"}]};
window.PREVIEW_DEEP = Object.assign(window.PREVIEW_DEEP || {}, {"nimhans-2026-recall": [{"q": "Which type of defence mechanism is dissociation?", "concept": "The answer is neurotic. The ranking comes from George Vaillant, the American psychiatrist who ran the Harvard Grant Study of adult development. In Adaptation to Life (1977) he took the defences that Sigmund Freud and Anna Freud (The Ego and the Mechanisms of Defence, 1936) had described and arranged them by maturity into four levels. Level I, psychotic: delusional projection, psychotic denial of external reality and distortion, where reality testing is lost. Level II, immature: projection, schizoid fantasy, hypochondriasis, passive aggression and acting out, normal in adolescence and common in personality disorders. Level III, neurotic (later called intermediate): repression, displacement, reaction formation, intellectualization (with isolation and undoing) and dissociation, which Vaillant also called neurotic denial. Level IV, mature: altruism, humour, sublimation, suppression and anticipation. Dissociation sits at the neurotic level because it is a temporary, drastic change in one's sense of identity or feeling to escape distress, while reality testing stays intact and the person keeps functioning. In the Grant Study, men who relied on mature defences had better health, marriages and careers decades later. The DSM-IV Defensive Functioning Scale (1994) keeps the same placement under another name: dissociation is at the mental inhibitions (compromise formation) level, with repression, displacement, intellectualization, isolation of affect, reaction formation and undoing. Hold the ladder with one anchor per level: distortion is psychotic, projection is immature, dissociation and repression are neurotic, suppression and sublimation are mature.", "traps": {"a": "Mature defences (altruism, humour, sublimation, suppression, anticipation) handle a conflict while keeping feelings, awareness and relationships intact. Dissociation cuts off part of awareness or identity, so it cannot be mature.", "b": "Immature defences are projection, acting out, passive aggression, hypochondriasis and schizoid fantasy. Dissociative disorders can look severe, which pulls people here, but Vaillant filed dissociation one level higher, as neurotic.", "d": "Defences are by definition unconscious. The one near-conscious exception is suppression, a mature defence (deciding to park a worry until later). Dissociation happens without any choice, so it is not conscious."}}, {"q": "A technique in which a response that inhibits anxiety is paired with an anxiety-provoking stimulus, step by step, to break the conditioned link between that stimulus and anxiety is:", "concept": "The answer is systematic desensitization, built by the South African psychiatrist Joseph Wolpe and set out in Psychotherapy by Reciprocal Inhibition (1958). Wolpe started with cats: he made them anxious with mild shocks in an experimental cage, then removed the anxiety by feeding them in rooms that looked less and less like that cage, so eating, a response incompatible with anxiety, gradually won. He borrowed Charles Sherrington's term reciprocal inhibition for the principle: if a response that inhibits anxiety is made in the presence of the anxiety-provoking stimulus, the bond between that stimulus and anxiety weakens. In people the incompatible response is usually deep muscle relaxation, taken from Edmund Jacobson's progressive relaxation (1938); Wolpe also used assertive and sexual responses. There are three steps. First, relaxation training. Second, an anxiety hierarchy: ten to twenty scenes ranked from least to most frightening on the Subjective Units of Disturbance scale (SUDS, 0 to 100). Third, desensitization proper: while relaxed, the client imagines the lowest scene, signals if anxiety rises, returns to relaxation, and moves up only when a scene can be held calmly. This is counterconditioning, replacing the conditioned response, not just waiting for extinction. The forerunner is Mary Cover Jones (1924), who removed little Peter's fear of a rabbit by bringing the rabbit nearer while he ate. In vivo desensitization uses the real stimulus instead of imagined scenes. Stem words that point here: gradual, hierarchy, relaxation, incompatible response, counterconditioning.", "traps": {"b": "Flooding puts the person in front of the most feared stimulus at full strength, with no hierarchy and no relaxation, and keeps them there until the anxiety dies out on its own. The stem's 'paired with a response that inhibits anxiety' and 'step by step' rule it out.", "c": "Aversion therapy pairs an unwanted behaviour (drinking, a paraphilic urge) with something unpleasant, such as an emetic or a shock, to attach discomfort to it. It adds a negative response; it does not remove anxiety.", "d": "Exposure and response prevention (Victor Meyer, 1966) is for OCD: face the obsession's trigger and block the compulsion. No incompatible response is paired with the stimulus; the anxiety is allowed to rise and fall on its own."}}, {"q": "In habit reversal training (HRT), the technique used to make the person aware of the habit is:", "concept": "The answer is awareness training, the first component of habit reversal training, which Nathan Azrin and Gregory Nunn published in 1973 ('Habit-reversal: a method of eliminating nervous habits and tics'). The logic is that tics, hair pulling, nail biting and skin picking run on autopilot, and you cannot block a habit you do not notice. Awareness training has four parts. Response description: the client describes the habit in detail, every movement, sometimes in front of a mirror. Response detection: the therapist points out each occurrence in session until the client catches every one himself. Early warning: the client learns to spot the earliest sign, the urge or the first movement (for hair pulling, the hand starting to rise toward the head). Situation awareness: listing the places, times, moods and people linked to the habit. Once the habit is caught early, the client uses the competing response, a physically incompatible action such as clenching the fists, held until the urge passes. Azrin and Nunn's original package had about a dozen techniques in four phases: awareness training, competing response training, habit control motivation (inconvenience review, social support, public display) and generalization training. Raymond Miltenberger, Wayne Fuqua and Douglas Woods (1998) showed that a simplified HRT works with just three: awareness training, competing response training and social support. The Comprehensive Behavioral Intervention for Tics (CBIT; John Piacentini and colleagues, 2010) is HRT plus function-based work and is a first-line treatment for Tourette's.", "traps": {"a": "Competing response training is the next step: once the habit is noticed, do an action that makes it physically impossible. It assumes awareness is already there, so it is not the technique that builds awareness.", "b": "Social support is a family member or friend who praises the client for using the competing response and prompts him when the habit appears. It keeps motivation up; it does not train the client's own detection of the habit.", "c": "Generalization training is the last phase of Azrin and Nunn's package: practising the new routine across situations, often in imagination, so it carries over beyond the clinic. It comes after awareness, not instead of it."}}, {"q": "The subjective confidence people have in their flashbulb memories is:", "concept": "The answer is very high confidence that stays high even as accuracy falls. Roger Brown and James Kulik coined the term flashbulb memory in 1977 for vivid, detailed memories of the circumstances in which a person heard shocking news, their main example being the assassination of John F. Kennedy in 1963. People recall where they were, what they were doing, who told them, how they felt, how others felt and what happened next (the canonical categories). Brown and Kulik thought a special 'Now Print!' mechanism, a term from Robert Livingston (1967), fixed these memories permanently. Later studies tested that by recording memories right after an event and re-testing later. Ulric Neisser and Nicole Harsch (1992) had students write down, the morning after, how they heard of the Challenger explosion (28 January 1986) and asked again about two and a half years later: accuracy was poor, about a quarter were wrong on every major detail, yet confidence stayed high, and some insisted the new version was right even when shown their own handwritten account. Jennifer Talarico and David Rubin (2003) recorded students' 9/11 memories on 12 September 2001 alongside an everyday memory. Over the following months both kinds lost consistency at the same rate, but only the flashbulb memories kept their vividness and the person's belief in their accuracy. Their conclusion is the exam line: flashbulb memories are special not in their accuracy but in the confidence attached to them. Emotional arousal at encoding (the amygdala) and endless retelling through media and conversation explain the vividness.", "traps": {"a": "The opposite is true. The emotional shock is exactly what makes these memories feel vivid and certain. Low confidence describes ordinary, forgettable memories, not flashbulb ones.", "c": "If confidence tracked accuracy there would be nothing special about flashbulb memories. The whole finding is the split: accuracy drops like any memory, confidence does not.", "d": "Talarico and Rubin (2003) found consistency fell over the months but belief in accuracy did not fall with it. Confidence staying high while accuracy drops is the key result, so 'falls in step' is the trap."}}, {"q": "An intervention in which the person deliberately does, or wishes for, the very opposite of the therapeutic goal in order to reach that goal is:", "concept": "The answer is paradoxical intention, a technique of Viktor Frankl, the Viennese psychiatrist who founded logotherapy. He first described it in 1939, and Man's Search for Meaning is the popular account of his ideas. Frankl noticed a loop he called anticipatory anxiety: a person fears a symptom (blushing, sweating, not falling asleep), the fear makes the symptom more likely, and its appearance strengthens the fear. Paradoxical intention breaks the loop by asking the person to intend, or even wish for, the very thing he fears, ideally with humour. The insomniac is told to try to stay awake as long as he can; the man afraid of sweating in public is told to show everyone how much he can really sweat. The moment you try to produce the symptom, the fear of it has nothing to work on, and the symptom usually fades. Frankl rooted this in self-detachment, the human capacity to step back from oneself and even laugh at oneself. He used it for phobias, obsessive fears and sleep problems, and behaviour therapists later tested it in trials, for example for sleep-onset insomnia (L. Michael Ascher and Ralph Turner, 1979). The family-therapy cousin is symptom prescription, used by Milton Erickson, Jay Haley and the Palo Alto group (Paul Watzlawick and colleagues, 1974), where the therapist instructs the family to keep doing the problem behaviour. The stem's cue, doing the opposite of the goal in order to reach the goal, is the textbook definition of paradoxical intention.", "traps": {"b": "Dereflection is Frankl's other technique, for hyper-reflection: the person is told to stop watching himself and turn attention outward, for example to a partner instead of his own sexual performance. It redirects attention; it does not ask for the opposite of the goal.", "c": "Negative practice (Knight Dunlap, 1928 and 1932) has the person perform an unwanted motor habit, such as a tic or a typing error, deliberately and over and over until fatigue makes it drop out. It repeats the habit; it is not about wishing for the feared outcome to defuse anticipatory anxiety. This is the closest trap.", "d": "Reciprocal inhibition is Joseph Wolpe's principle behind systematic desensitization: pair the feared stimulus with an incompatible response such as relaxation. Nothing in it asks the person to aim at the opposite of the goal."}}, {"q": "In ICD-11, gender incongruence is placed in:", "concept": "The answer is Chapter 17, Conditions related to sexual health. ICD-11 was adopted by the World Health Assembly in May 2019 and came into effect on 1 January 2022. In ICD-10 the topic sat in Chapter V (mental and behavioural disorders) as F64, gender identity disorders, which included transsexualism (F64.0), dual-role transvestism (F64.1) and gender identity disorder of childhood (F64.2). ICD-11 renamed it gender incongruence and moved it out of the mental disorders chapter into a new Chapter 17, Conditions related to sexual health. WHO's reasoning: being transgender is not a mental illness and filing it under mental disorders added stigma, but keeping a code keeps access to gender-affirming health care, which health systems and insurers usually pay for only when there is a code. ICD-11 has two codes. HA60, gender incongruence of adolescence or adulthood: a marked and persistent incongruence between experienced gender and assigned sex, often with a wish to transition, present for several months. HA61, gender incongruence of childhood: in children before puberty, persisting for about two years. Gender-variant behaviour and preferences alone are not enough for either. Sexual dysfunctions and sexual pain disorders moved to Chapter 17 too, while paraphilic disorders stayed in Chapter 6. DSM-5 (2013) and DSM-5-TR (2022) do not follow ICD here: they keep the condition in the manual as gender dysphoria, where the diagnosis rests on clinically significant distress or impairment.", "traps": {"a": "Chapter 6 is the successor of ICD-10's Chapter V, where gender identity disorders (F64) used to sit, which is why it is the trap. ICD-11 moved gender incongruence out of it on purpose.", "b": "Hormone treatment is part of gender-affirming care, which makes the endocrine chapter tempting, but gender incongruence is not filed as an endocrine disease. Its home is Chapter 17.", "d": "WHO kept a code deliberately, because without one, gender-affirming care is hard to get funded. It was renamed and relocated, not deleted."}}, {"q": "Bavisha lost her job. Since then she is stressed, has stopped eating properly and cannot get herself to do anything. According to yogic philosophy, the main issue she is dealing with is:", "concept": "The answer is tamas alone. The theory of the three gunas (triguna) comes from Samkhya philosophy, traditionally traced to the sage Kapila and set out in Ishvarakrishna's Samkhya Karika, and it runs through the Bhagavad Gita (chapters 14, 17 and 18). Prakriti, the material nature that includes the mind, is made of three gunas (strands or qualities). All three are always present in different proportions, and whichever dominates colours a person's thinking, feeling and behaviour. Sattva is light, clarity, calm, knowledge and contentment. Rajas is activity, passion, desire, restlessness, attachment, anger and greed. Tamas is darkness, heaviness, inertia, dullness, delusion, excess sleep and the inability to act. Bavisha after losing her job is not driven, angry or racing about, which would be rajas; she is stuck, withdrawn, not eating and not functioning, which is tamas dominating. In Indian psychology writing a tamas-heavy state is the closest match to depression (low energy, slowing, withdrawal), rajas to anxious, agitated or manic states, and sattva to mental health. Treatment in this frame means reducing tamas and building sattva: routine, activity, sattvic food, yoga and meditation. One research measure is the Vedic Personality Inventory by David Wolf (1998). Yama, the other option word, is not a guna or a mental state at all; it is the first of Patanjali's eight limbs of yoga, the five ethical restraints. Your recall had 'Yama alone' and 'Tamas alone' as options, so the other two here are rebuilt.", "traps": {"a": "Yama is the first limb of Patanjali's ashtanga yoga: five ethical restraints (non-violence, truthfulness, non-stealing, sexual restraint, non-possessiveness). It is a discipline you practise, not a condition you suffer from, so it cannot be her problem.", "c": "Since yama is not a problem state, adding it to tamas makes the option wrong. 'Both' options are where careful reading wins marks.", "d": "Rajas is the guna of over-activity, craving, restlessness and anger. Stress can look rajasic, but her picture is shutdown and inertia, not agitation, so tamas fits better."}}, {"q": "Which of the eight limbs of yoga is concentration, directing the mind's energy to a single point?", "concept": "The answer is dharana. Patanjali's Yoga Sutras (compiled somewhere between about 200 BCE and 400 CE) define yoga in sutra 1.2 as 'yogas chitta vritti nirodhah', the stilling of the fluctuations of the mind, and lay out an eightfold path, ashtanga yoga, in sutra 2.29: (1) yama, ethical restraints; (2) niyama, personal observances; (3) asana, posture; (4) pranayama, breath regulation; (5) pratyahara, withdrawal of the senses from their objects; (6) dharana, concentration; (7) dhyana, meditation; (8) samadhi, absorption. Sutra 3.1 defines dharana as binding the mind to one place (desha bandhah chittasya): holding attention on a single point or object, such as the breath, a spot between the eyebrows or a candle flame. That is exactly 'concentrating' and 'directing mental energy to one point in the mind', the two wordings in your paper. Dhyana (3.2) is what dharana becomes when the focus flows unbroken, like oil poured from one vessel to another, without effort. Samadhi (3.3) is when only the object shines and the sense of a separate meditator drops away. Dharana, dhyana and samadhi practised together on one object are called samyama (3.4); these three are the inner limbs (antaranga), and the first five are the outer limbs (bahiranga). Fast ladder: pratyahara pulls the senses in, dharana points the mind, dhyana keeps it pointed, samadhi merges with the point. Your recall had pratyahara and jnana as options; the fourth is rebuilt.", "traps": {"a": "Pratyahara, the fifth limb, is withdrawing the senses from outside objects and turning attention inward. It clears the ground for concentration but is not concentration itself. This is the classic neighbour trap.", "b": "Jnana yoga is the path of knowledge, one of the paths in the Bhagavad Gita alongside karma (action) and bhakti (devotion), with raja yoga often added as a fourth. It is not one of Patanjali's eight limbs.", "c": "Dhyana, the seventh limb, is meditation: concentration that has become an unbroken, effortless flow. The question asks for the act of fixing the mind on one point, which comes first and is dharana."}}, {"q": "Kegel exercises strengthen which part of the body?", "concept": "The answer is the pelvic floor muscles. Arnold Kegel, an American gynaecologist, described the exercises in 1948 as a non-surgical treatment for women with urinary incontinence after childbirth. The pelvic floor is the hammock of muscle across the bottom of the pelvis, mainly the levator ani group, whose pubococcygeus part (the PC muscle) runs from the pubic bone to the tailbone. It supports the bladder, bowel and uterus and controls the urethral and anal openings. A Kegel is a squeeze-and-lift of these muscles (the ones used to stop urine midstream, though doing that routinely is not advised), held for a few seconds and then relaxed, repeated in sets through the day. Kegel used a pressure gauge placed in the vagina, the perineometer, so the patient could see her own squeeze, which makes his method one of the earliest forms of biofeedback. Uses today: stress urinary incontinence (leaking on coughing, sneezing or lifting), recovery after childbirth, pelvic organ prolapse, incontinence in men after prostate surgery, and faecal incontinence. The psychology link, and the reason it turns up in entrance papers, is twofold. In health psychology and behavioural medicine, biofeedback-assisted pelvic floor training is a standard behavioural treatment. In sex therapy, pelvic floor exercises are part of the directed masturbation programme for female orgasmic disorder (Joseph LoPiccolo and W. Charles Lobitz, 1972) and are used as an add-on for premature ejaculation, while pelvic floor relaxation work with graded dilators is used for vaginismus.", "traps": {"a": "The abdominal muscles (rectus abdominis, obliques, transversus abdominis) are trained by core work such as planks. They work alongside the pelvic floor but are not what a Kegel targets.", "c": "The lumbar extensors are trained in back-strengthening programmes for low back pain. A Kegel involves no back movement at all.", "d": "Squeezing the buttocks is the commonest mistake people make when trying a Kegel. The glutes move the hip; the pelvic floor sits deeper, between the pubic bone and the tailbone."}}, {"q": "In George Vaillant's hierarchy, which one of these is a mature defence?", "concept": "The answer is suppression, the only one of the four that Vaillant (1977) placed at Level IV, the mature level, with altruism, humour, sublimation and anticipation. Suppression is a conscious or semi-conscious decision to postpone attention to a worry: 'I will deal with the result after the exam.' The feeling is not denied or distorted; it is parked and picked up later. That is why it is called adaptive: the person stays aware of reality and keeps functioning. The near-identical word repression is the trap. Repression, Freud's original and central defence, is unconscious: the threatening thought or memory is kept out of awareness without the person choosing it, and it tends to return in disguised forms such as slips, symptoms and dreams. Vaillant put repression at Level III, neurotic. A useful pairing: suppression is conscious and mature, repression is unconscious and neurotic. The other mature defences are altruism (getting satisfaction from helping others with the very thing that troubles you), humour (seeing the funny side of a painful situation without hurting anyone), sublimation (channelling an unacceptable impulse into a valued activity, as in turning aggression into competitive sport) and anticipation (realistic planning for future discomfort). In the Grant Study, Harvard men followed from the late 1930s, mature defences at midlife predicted better physical health, income, marriage and enjoyment of life decades later. Exam stems often describe a person and ask for the defence: 'decides not to think about the bill until payday' is suppression; 'has no memory of the accident' is repression.", "traps": {"a": "Repression is the unconscious exclusion of a thought or memory from awareness, Freud's primary defence. Vaillant put it at Level III, neurotic. Same-looking word as suppression, opposite end of the ladder.", "b": "Projection, attributing your own unacceptable feelings to someone else, is a Level II immature defence, common in paranoid and other personality disorders.", "c": "Reaction formation, showing the opposite of what you feel (fussing over a sibling you resent), is Level III, neurotic."}}, {"q": "Which group contains only immature defences in Vaillant's (1977) scheme?", "concept": "The answer is projection, acting out, passive aggression and hypochondriasis, four of the five Level II immature defences (the fifth is schizoid fantasy). Vaillant called them immature because they are normal in adolescence but, when they persist in adults, they distort how others are seen and damage relationships; they are the defences most often seen in personality disorders and substance misuse. Projection: attributing your own unacceptable feelings to someone else ('he hates me' when you resent him). Acting out: discharging an impulse directly in action without reflecting on the feeling behind it (getting drunk and picking a fight after a rejection). Passive aggression: expressing hostility indirectly, through procrastination, sulking, 'forgetting' or failing on purpose. Hypochondriasis: turning reproach toward others into complaints of physical illness. Schizoid fantasy: retreating into an inner fantasy world to avoid intimacy. The other three options are the other three levels as complete blocks. Repression, displacement, reaction formation and intellectualization are Level III, neurotic, the level where dissociation also sits. Altruism, humour, sublimation and anticipation are Level IV, mature, together with suppression. Delusional projection, psychotic denial and distortion are Level I, psychotic. Learn each level as a block, because papers either name one defence and ask its level, as your NIMHANS paper did with dissociation, or list four and ask which one does not belong. One trap to watch: projection is immature, but delusional projection, where the projected belief becomes a fixed delusion, is psychotic.", "traps": {"b": "These four are Level III, neurotic defences: they cause private distress but the person functions and reality testing is intact.", "c": "These are Level IV, mature defences, the healthiest end of the ladder.", "d": "These are Level I, psychotic defences, where reality itself is reshaped to fit the wish. Note that delusional projection is psychotic while plain projection is immature."}}, {"q": "In systematic desensitization, the 0 to 100 scale on which the client rates how anxious each scene in the hierarchy makes him is the:", "concept": "The answer is SUDS, the Subjective Units of Disturbance (often called Distress) Scale, introduced by Joseph Wolpe in The Practice of Behavior Therapy (1969). The client rates his own anxiety from 0 (completely calm) to 100 (the worst anxiety he can imagine). SUDS is used twice in desensitization. First, to build the hierarchy: each feared situation gets a SUDS rating and the scenes are ordered with fairly even steps between them, often 5 to 10 points, so no jump is too big. Second, during treatment: after the client imagines a scene while relaxed he reports his SUDS, and the therapist moves up the hierarchy only when the current scene has come down close to zero. SUDS is a quick idiographic measure, made for one person in the moment, not a standardised test with norms. It is now used across exposure therapies, in EMDR (Francine Shapiro) and in exposure and response prevention for OCD. A typical hierarchy for a fear of injections starts around 10 with reading the word 'injection', runs through about 40 for sitting in a clinic waiting room and about 70 for watching someone else get an injection, and ends at 100 with getting one yourself. Two other Wolpe items worth knowing: the Fear Survey Schedule (with Peter Lang, 1964), used at the start to map what the client fears, and assertiveness training, which he also used as an anxiety-inhibiting response. If a stem says 'subjective', '0 to 100' and 'hierarchy' together, it is SUDS.", "traps": {"a": "The Fear Survey Schedule (Joseph Wolpe and Peter Lang, 1964) is a questionnaire listing many feared objects and situations, used at assessment to find out what the client fears. It is Wolpe's too, which is the trap, but it does not rate scenes from 0 to 100 during the hierarchy.", "b": "The State-Trait Anxiety Inventory (Charles Spielberger) is a standardised questionnaire with 20 state and 20 trait items, each scale scored 20 to 80. It is not a moment-by-moment 0 to 100 rating.", "d": "The Hamilton Anxiety Rating Scale (Max Hamilton, 1959) is a clinician-rated 14-item scale of anxiety severity. The clinician scores it, not the client, and it is not used to rank scenes."}}, {"q": "Flooding differs from systematic desensitization mainly because flooding:", "concept": "The answer is full-intensity exposure with no hierarchy and no relaxation. Both techniques rest on conditioning, but on different mechanisms. Systematic desensitization (Joseph Wolpe, 1958) is counterconditioning: anxiety is replaced by an incompatible response, relaxation, one rung of the hierarchy at a time. Flooding is extinction: the person is kept in front of the most feared stimulus, in real life or in imagination, and prevented from escaping until the anxiety peaks and falls on its own. Escape is what keeps a phobia alive. O. Hobart Mowrer's two-factor theory (1947) explains why: fear is learned by classical conditioning and maintained by operant avoidance, because every escape is negatively reinforced by relief, so the person never stays long enough to learn that the stimulus is harmless. Block the escape and that learning finally happens. Implosive therapy (Thomas Stampfl and Donald Levis, 1967) is flooding in imagination, with scenes exaggerated and laced with psychodynamic themes. Flooding is faster but harder to tolerate and has more drop-outs; graded exposure sits in between and is what most CBT uses today. Aversion therapy is a different family altogether: it pairs an unwanted behaviour with something unpleasant to create an aversion rather than remove a fear. Reading the stem: 'maximum intensity', 'no escape' or 'prolonged exposure until anxiety subsides' is flooding; 'gradual', 'hierarchy' or 'relaxation' is desensitization; 'pairing with nausea or shock' is aversion therapy.", "traps": {"a": "Pairing with a shock or an emetic is aversion therapy, used to make an unwanted behaviour unpleasant. Neither flooding nor desensitization adds an aversive stimulus.", "b": "That is systematic desensitization itself, so it cannot be what makes flooding different.", "c": "Flooding can be done in vivo (the real stimulus) or in imagination; implosive therapy is the imaginal form. Desensitization was originally imaginal, so imagination is not what separates them."}}, {"q": "In habit reversal training, a good competing response must be:", "concept": "The answer is a response that is physically incompatible with the habit, socially inconspicuous and held for about a minute or until the urge passes. In Nathan Azrin and Gregory Nunn's habit reversal (1973), the competing response is the active ingredient that follows awareness training. Once the client notices the habit or the urge, he does something that makes the habit impossible at that moment, usually by tensing the opposing muscles. Examples: for hair pulling or nail biting, clench the fists or hold an object; for a head-jerk tic, gently tense the neck muscles while keeping the head still; for an eye-blink tic, slow deliberate blinking; for a vocal tic, slow rhythmic breathing through the nose. It must be inconspicuous so it can be used in class or on a bus without embarrassment, and it is practised until it becomes the automatic reaction to the urge. Azrin and Nunn had clients hold it for three minutes; later versions use about one minute. Why it works is still debated: it physically blocks the habit, interrupts the habit chain, and lets the urge fade without being rewarded. For tics, the urge is a premonitory sensation that the tic relieves, so each tic is negatively reinforced; riding out the urge with a competing response breaks that cycle. Social support, a parent or partner praising use of the competing response and prompting it, completes the simplified version of HRT (Miltenberger, Fuqua and Woods, 1998).", "traps": {"a": "That is punishment, for example overcorrection or snapping a rubber band. HRT is built on noticing and replacing the habit, not on punishing it.", "c": "That is Knight Dunlap's negative practice (massed practice), an older technique for tics. HRT does the opposite: it blocks the habit.", "d": "Relaxation can be an add-on, but a once-a-day exercise does nothing at the moment the urge strikes. The competing response must be used every time, right then."}}, {"q": "Habit reversal training is a first-line behavioural treatment for all of the following EXCEPT:", "concept": "The answer is obsessive-compulsive disorder, whose first-line psychological treatment is exposure and response prevention (ERP), which grew out of Victor Meyer's work in 1966 and was developed further by Edna Foa, usually alongside SSRIs. HRT targets a different family: tics and body-focused repetitive behaviours (BFRBs), the habits aimed at one's own body such as hair pulling (trichotillomania), skin picking (excoriation disorder), nail biting and cheek chewing. The trap is the DSM-5 (2013) chapter layout: trichotillomania and excoriation disorder sit in the obsessive-compulsive and related disorders chapter, right next to OCD, so it is easy to assume they share a treatment. They do not. In OCD the compulsion is driven by an obsession (a thought such as 'my hands are contaminated') and is done to neutralise the anxiety that thought creates. In BFRBs and tics there is usually no obsession behind the act, just an urge, tension or sensation that the habit relieves, often with little awareness. That is why HRT starts with awareness training and a competing response, while ERP exposes the person to the obsession's trigger and blocks the ritual. For tics, the Comprehensive Behavioral Intervention for Tics (CBIT; John Piacentini and colleagues, 2010, a randomised trial in children published in JAMA) is HRT plus function-based work and is recommended first-line. On an EXCEPT stem, three options are HRT targets and you are hunting for the outlier, so read every option before marking.", "traps": {"a": "Hair pulling is a textbook HRT target. Azrin and Nunn's 1973 sample included hair pullers, and HRT is still the first-line psychological treatment for it.", "b": "HRT, and CBIT built on it, are first-line behavioural treatments for tic disorders and Tourette's.", "d": "Skin picking is a body-focused repetitive behaviour treated with HRT, exactly like hair pulling."}}, {"q": "Talarico and Rubin (2003) compared students' 9/11 memories with their everyday memories. The flashbulb memories differed in:", "concept": "The answer is vividness and belief in their accuracy, not consistency. Jennifer Talarico and David Rubin at Duke University had 54 students record, on 12 September 2001, how they first heard about the attacks, along with a recent everyday memory (a party, a study session) as a control. Each student was tested again either 1, 6 or 32 weeks later. Both kinds of memory became less consistent at the same rate: details changed, dropped out or were added. What differed was the experience of remembering. For the flashbulb memories, ratings of vividness, of reliving the event and of belief in the memory's accuracy stayed high, while for everyday memories they fell. The paper's title says it all: 'Confidence, not consistency, characterizes flashbulb memories'. This fits Ulric Neisser and Nicole Harsch's Challenger study (1992) and goes against Roger Brown and James Kulik's (1977) idea of a special 'Now Print!' mechanism that would preserve these memories exactly. The modern view is that flashbulb memories are ordinary memories made to feel special by strong emotion at encoding and by heavy rehearsal, since the event is replayed in the media and retold again and again, and every retelling can reshape the memory. A large national 9/11 survey led by William Hirst (2009, with a ten-year follow-up in 2015) found the same pattern: consistency dropped in the first year and then levelled off, while confidence stayed high throughout. One-line version for the exam: high confidence, falling accuracy.", "traps": {"b": "This is what Brown and Kulik's theory predicted, and it is exactly what Talarico and Rubin did not find: flashbulb and everyday memories lost consistency at the same rate.", "c": "Flashbulb memories were not near perfect; at 32 weeks they were as inconsistent as everyday memories. Only the confidence held up.", "d": "Details were lost at a similar rate for both kinds of memory. Flashbulb memories were not forgotten faster; they just felt more vivid."}}, {"q": "Current research explains the vividness of flashbulb memories mainly by:", "concept": "The answer is emotional arousal at encoding plus repeated rehearsal. When Roger Brown and James Kulik named flashbulb memories in 1977 they proposed a special neural 'Now Print!' mechanism, borrowed from Robert Livingston (1967), that switched on for surprising, consequential events and fixed the whole scene like a photograph. The diary studies that followed (Neisser and Harsch, 1992; Talarico and Rubin, 2003) showed these memories drift like any other, so no special mechanism is needed. Two ordinary processes explain the vividness. The first is emotion at encoding: arousal releases adrenaline and cortisol, the amygdala signals the hippocampus, and consolidation of the central emotional details is strengthened (James McGaugh's work on emotional memory). Arousal also narrows attention to the central event, the same narrowing behind Elizabeth Loftus's weapon focus, so peripheral details such as who told you are encoded weakly, and those are exactly the details flashbulb studies find inconsistent. The second is rehearsal: such events are replayed on television and talked about for weeks, and each retelling rebuilds the memory, sometimes mixing in things learned later. Kathy Pezdek (2003) found that most students she surveyed said they had seen the first plane hit the World Trade Center on live television on 11 September, although that footage was not broadcast until the next day. Neisser called such memories benchmarks: we keep them vivid because they tie our own life story to history. Exam pairing: Brown and Kulik 1977, special mechanism and high confidence; Neisser and Harsch 1992 and Talarico and Rubin 2003, accuracy falls while confidence does not.", "traps": {"a": "This was Brown and Kulik's 1977 proposal. The diary studies that followed showed these memories change over time like any other, so the special-mechanism idea has been dropped.", "b": "Flashbulb events get more interference than most, from endless media coverage and retelling, which is one reason the details drift.", "c": "Eidetic (photographic) imagery is a rare ability, found mostly in children, and has nothing to do with flashbulb memories, which almost everyone forms."}}, {"q": "A man with sleep-onset insomnia is told to lie in bed with the lights off and try as hard as he can to stay awake. This technique is:", "concept": "The answer is paradoxical intention, Viktor Frankl's technique applied to sleep. Sleep-onset insomnia is often kept going by performance anxiety about sleep: the harder a person tries to fall asleep, the more aroused he becomes, because sleep is one of the few things effort cannot produce. Frankl's anticipatory-anxiety loop runs here: fear of another sleepless night produces arousal, the arousal produces the sleepless night, and that strengthens the fear. Telling him to try to stay awake removes the effort to sleep, so the performance anxiety has nothing to feed on, and sleep tends to come. Behaviour therapists tested it in controlled trials, for example L. Michael Ascher and Ralph Turner (1979), and it is one of the recognised psychological treatments for insomnia, usually as a component within cognitive behavioural therapy for insomnia (CBT-I), whose core parts are stimulus control, sleep restriction, sleep hygiene, relaxation and cognitive therapy. Tell the CBT-I parts apart by the instruction given. Stimulus control (Richard Bootzin, 1972): use the bed only for sleep and sex, go to bed only when sleepy, get up and leave the room if not asleep in about 20 minutes, keep a fixed rising time, no naps. Sleep restriction (Arthur Spielman, 1987): limit time in bed to the time actually spent asleep to build sleep pressure, then extend it gradually. Sleep hygiene: caffeine, alcohol, exercise timing, a dark and quiet room. Paradoxical intention: stay in bed and try to remain awake. The stem gives the last instruction.", "traps": {"b": "Stimulus control (Richard Bootzin, 1972) would tell him to get up and leave the bedroom if he cannot sleep, to re-link the bed with sleep. It never asks him to stay awake in bed.", "c": "Sleep restriction (Arthur Spielman, 1987) cuts the time allowed in bed to build sleep drive. It limits time in bed; it does not ask him to try not to sleep.", "d": "Dereflection would turn his attention away from sleep onto something else meaningful. The stem asks him to aim directly at the opposite of the goal, which is paradoxical intention."}}, {"q": "Frankl's technique for 'hyper-reflection', in which a person stops watching himself and turns his attention outward, for example from his own sexual performance to his partner, is:", "concept": "The answer is dereflection. Viktor Frankl described two faulty patterns that keep symptoms going. Hyper-intention is trying too hard for something that can only come as a by-product, like sleep, orgasm or happiness. Hyper-reflection is excessive attention to oneself: watching one's own body, feelings or performance. His two techniques match them. Paradoxical intention handles anticipatory anxiety, and dereflection handles hyper-reflection. In dereflection the person is helped to ignore the symptom and redirect attention outward, to another person, a task or a meaning, so that self-observation stops interfering. Frankl's classic examples were sexual: a man whose erection fails because he is monitoring it, or a woman who cannot reach orgasm because she is watching for it, is told to stop striving and give attention to the partner. Logotherapy, Frankl's school, is often called the third Viennese school of psychotherapy, after Sigmund Freud's psychoanalysis and Alfred Adler's individual psychology. It holds that the primary human motive is the will to meaning (against Freud's will to pleasure and Adler's will to power), and that a lack of meaning produces an existential vacuum and what Frankl called noogenic neurosis. Its methods are paradoxical intention, dereflection and Socratic dialogue to help the client discover meaning. His own years in Nazi concentration camps are told in Man's Search for Meaning. Exam rule: fear of a symptom, paradoxical intention; excessive self-watching, dereflection.", "traps": {"a": "Paradoxical intention asks the person to wish for or try to produce the feared symptom. The stem asks for attention moved away from the self, which is dereflection.", "b": "Sensate focus (William Masters and Virginia Johnson, 1970) is a graded touching programme in sex therapy that removes the demand to perform. It also reduces spectatoring, which is why it tempts, but it is Masters and Johnson's, not Frankl's.", "d": "Socratic dialogue in logotherapy is questioning that helps the client uncover meaning in his own life. It is Frankl's, but it is not the attention-shifting technique."}}, {"q": "When gender incongruence moved out of the mental disorders chapter in ICD-11, which of these stayed in Chapter 6 (Mental, behavioural or neurodevelopmental disorders)?", "concept": "The answer is paraphilic disorders. ICD-11 created Chapter 17, Conditions related to sexual health, and moved three groups into it from the old mental and behavioural chapter: gender incongruence (HA60 for adolescence or adulthood, HA61 for childhood), sexual dysfunctions (HA00 to HA03: hypoactive sexual desire dysfunction, sexual arousal dysfunctions, orgasmic dysfunctions and ejaculatory dysfunctions) and sexual pain disorders (sexual pain-penetration disorder, HA20, which replaces vaginismus and dyspareunia). WHO's reasoning: these conditions are not best understood as mental disorders, and ICD-10 had split them on an outdated mind-body line, with non-organic sexual dysfunctions in the mental chapter and organic ones elsewhere. ICD-11 puts them in one place and lets the clinician add aetiological qualifiers instead (associated with a medical condition, with psychological or behavioural factors, with a substance or medication, with lack of knowledge, and so on). Paraphilic disorders stayed in Chapter 6 (6D30 to 6D3Z: exhibitionistic, voyeuristic, pedophilic, coercive sexual sadism and frotteuristic disorders, plus other paraphilic disorders) because they centre on acting on, or being markedly distressed by, sexual arousal involving people who do not or cannot consent. ICD-11 also dropped several old categories as not disorders, such as fetishism, fetishistic transvestism and sadomasochism between consenting adults. Chapter numbers worth holding: 6 is mental disorders, 7 is sleep-wake disorders (which also moved out of the mental chapter), 17 is sexual health.", "traps": {"a": "Sexual dysfunctions moved to Chapter 17 along with gender incongruence, with aetiology qualifiers replacing ICD-10's organic versus non-organic split.", "c": "Sexual pain-penetration disorder (HA20) is also in Chapter 17. It replaced ICD-10's non-organic vaginismus and dyspareunia.", "d": "Gender incongruence of childhood (HA61) moved with the adult code into Chapter 17. Neither form stayed in Chapter 6."}}, {"q": "The DSM-5 (2013) diagnosis that corresponds to ICD-11 gender incongruence is:", "concept": "The answer is gender dysphoria. The label has changed with almost every edition, and each change shows the thinking of its time. DSM-III (1980) introduced transsexualism (for adults) and gender identity disorder of childhood. DSM-IV (1994) merged them into gender identity disorder, which framed the identity itself as the disorder. DSM-5 (2013) renamed it gender dysphoria and moved the focus to distress: the diagnosis is about the clinically significant distress or impairment that comes from the incongruence between one's experienced or expressed gender and assigned gender, not about being transgender as such. DSM-5 criteria: a marked incongruence lasting at least 6 months. Adolescents and adults need at least 2 of 6 indicators, such as a strong desire to be rid of one's sex characteristics or to be treated as another gender. Children need at least 6 of 8, one of which must be a strong desire to be of another gender or an insistence that one is. Gender dysphoria has its own chapter in DSM-5, separate from sexual dysfunctions and paraphilic disorders. DSM-5-TR (2022) kept the diagnosis and updated the wording, for example 'experienced gender' instead of 'desired gender' and 'gender-affirming medical procedure' instead of 'cross-sex medical procedure'. ICD-11 went further: it calls the condition gender incongruence, requires no distress for the diagnosis, and placed it outside the mental disorders chapter. So the DSM keeps it as a mental disorder defined by distress, and ICD-11 keeps a code outside mental disorders for access to care.", "traps": {"a": "Gender identity disorder was the DSM-IV (1994) name. DSM-5 dropped it because it labelled the identity itself as disordered.", "b": "Transsexualism was the DSM-III (1980) term and ICD-10's F64.0. Neither DSM-5 nor ICD-11 uses it.", "c": "Gender incongruence is the ICD-11 term (HA60, HA61). The question asks for DSM-5's term, which is gender dysphoria."}}, {"q": "In the triguna theory, the guna marked by restlessness, passion, desire and over-activity is:", "concept": "The answer is rajas. The three gunas are best learned as a table of opposites, because exam stems describe a person and ask which guna dominates. Sattva: light, clear, calm, balanced, truthful, content, wise; the sattvic person is self-controlled and helpful, and sattva is linked with mental health and wisdom. Rajas: active, passionate, ambitious, restless, craving, attached; under stress it turns into anger, greed, anxiety, irritability and the endless chase of pleasure, success or status. Tamas: dark, heavy, dull, inert, deluded, sleepy, negligent; the tamasic person is lazy, confused and stuck. In one line: rajas is energy that moves, tamas is energy that is blocked, sattva is energy in balance. The Bhagavad Gita (chapter 14) says sattva binds a person through attachment to happiness and knowledge, rajas through attachment to action, and tamas through negligence, laziness and sleep; chapter 17 extends the scheme to food, worship and giving (sattvic food is fresh and light, rajasic food spicy and stimulating, tamasic food stale or heavy). All three are present in everyone; personality is the balance, and the balance shifts with lifestyle and practice. The clinical mapping used in Indian psychology: excess rajas looks like anxiety, agitation, anger or mania; excess tamas looks like depression, apathy and lethargy. Quick translation for scenario stems: hyper and craving is rajas, stuck and dull is tamas, calm and clear is sattva. Your Bavisha question was the tamas version of this.", "traps": {"a": "Sattva is calm, clarity and balance, the opposite of restlessness.", "c": "Tamas is inertia, dullness and lethargy, the opposite of over-activity.", "d": "Ahamkara is the ego, the 'I-maker', one of the products of prakriti in Samkhya; with buddhi (intellect) and manas (mind) it forms the antahkarana, the inner instrument. It is not a guna."}}, {"q": "The concept of the three gunas comes mainly from which school of Indian philosophy, alongside the Bhagavad Gita?", "concept": "The answer is Samkhya, one of the six orthodox (astika) schools of Indian philosophy, traditionally founded by the sage Kapila; its oldest surviving text is Ishvarakrishna's Samkhya Karika (around the 4th to 5th century CE). Samkhya is a dualism of two eternal realities. Purusha is pure consciousness: the witness, unchanging and inactive. Prakriti is primal matter, which includes not only the body and the world but also the mind. Prakriti is made of the three gunas, sattva, rajas and tamas. When they are in perfect balance nothing manifests; when the balance is disturbed in the presence of purusha, prakriti unfolds into the world in a fixed order: buddhi (intellect) first, then ahamkara (ego), then manas (mind) with the five senses, the five organs of action, the five subtle elements and the five gross elements, 23 products in all, or 25 principles (tattvas) counting purusha and prakriti. Suffering comes from purusha wrongly identifying with the workings of prakriti, and liberation (kaivalya) is the discriminating knowledge that separates the two. Yoga is Samkhya's partner school: Patanjali takes over Samkhya's metaphysics, adds Ishvara (a special purusha) and a practical method, the eight limbs. The six orthodox schools are usually paired: Samkhya with Yoga, Nyaya with Vaisheshika, Mimamsa with Vedanta. Psychology papers use Samkhya for the gunas, for purusha and prakriti, and for the antahkarana (buddhi, ahamkara and manas).", "traps": {"a": "Nyaya, founded by Gautama (Akshapada), is the school of logic and of valid means of knowledge (pramanas). The gunas are not its framework.", "b": "Charvaka (Lokayata) is the materialist, heterodox school that accepts only perception as a source of knowledge and rejects the soul. There are no gunas in it.", "d": "Purva Mimamsa (Jaimini) interprets Vedic ritual and duty (dharma). The gunas come from Samkhya."}}, {"q": "During practice, a student learns to stop her attention being pulled by the sounds, smells and sights around her and to turn her senses inward. Which limb of yoga is this?", "concept": "The answer is pratyahara, the fifth of Patanjali's eight limbs. The word means drawing back (prati, against, and ahara, food or what is taken in): the senses stop feeding on their objects and are drawn inward, the way a tortoise pulls in its limbs, which is the Bhagavad Gita's image (2.58). Patanjali's sutra 2.54 describes it as the senses withdrawing from their objects and following the mind inward. Pratyahara is the hinge of ashtanga yoga. The first four limbs (yama, niyama, asana, pranayama) work on conduct, body and breath; the last three (dharana, dhyana, samadhi) work on the mind; pratyahara is the bridge, since a mind still pulled by every noise cannot concentrate. So the sequence a stem may walk you through is: first switch off the outside (pratyahara), then fix on one point (dharana), then hold it in an unbroken flow (dhyana), then absorption (samadhi). This is the exact pair that was set against each other in your paper: 'withdrawal of the senses' is pratyahara, 'concentration on one point' or 'directing mental energy to one point' is dharana. Lock it as senses in, pratyahara; mind fixed, dharana. The other options in this question are earlier limbs: pranayama is breath regulation (the fourth limb), and niyama is the five observances (the second limb: shaucha or cleanliness, santosha or contentment, tapas or austerity, svadhyaya or self-study, ishvara pranidhana or surrender to the Lord).", "traps": {"b": "Dharana, the sixth limb, is fixing the mind on one point. It comes after the senses have been withdrawn, so it is the next step, not this one.", "c": "Pranayama, the fourth limb, is control of the breath. Breath work calms the system, but the stem is about withdrawing the senses.", "d": "Niyama, the second limb, is the five personal observances: cleanliness, contentment, austerity, self-study and surrender to Ishvara. It is about conduct, not the senses."}}, {"q": "Which is the correct order of the last four of Patanjali's eight limbs?", "concept": "The answer is pratyahara, dharana, dhyana, samadhi. Patanjali's full list (Yoga Sutra 2.29) is yama, niyama, asana, pranayama, pratyahara, dharana, dhyana, samadhi. A line to hold it: Yogis Never Argue, Peaceful People Don't Disturb Silence. The order is a progression from outer to inner, and each limb prepares the next. Yama (the five restraints: ahimsa or non-violence, satya or truthfulness, asteya or non-stealing, brahmacharya or sexual restraint, aparigraha or non-possessiveness) and niyama (the five observances: shaucha, santosha, tapas, svadhyaya and ishvara pranidhana) settle conduct. Asana steadies the body; sutra 2.46 says posture should be steady and comfortable (sthira sukham asanam). Pranayama regulates the breath. Pratyahara withdraws the senses. Then come the three inner limbs: dharana fixes the mind on one point (3.1), dhyana is that focus flowing unbroken (3.2), and samadhi is absorption in which only the object remains (3.3). Practised together on one object, the three are called samyama (3.4). The first five are the outer limbs (bahiranga), the last three the inner limbs (antaranga). The pairs that get swapped are the ones that sound alike. Pranayama and pratyahara both start with 'pra': breath comes before the senses. Dharana and dhyana both start with 'dh': fixing comes before flowing. If you can place those two pairs, the order question is free.", "traps": {"a": "Concentration cannot come before the senses are withdrawn. Pratyahara (5th) comes before dharana (6th).", "c": "Flow comes out of focus, not the other way round. Dharana (6th) comes before dhyana (7th).", "d": "This reverses the inner sequence. The order runs from outer to inner: pratyahara, dharana, dhyana, samadhi."}}, {"q": "Kegel (pelvic floor) exercises are a standard first-line treatment for:", "concept": "The answer is stress urinary incontinence after childbirth, the condition Arnold Kegel designed the exercises for in 1948. Stress incontinence is leakage when pressure in the abdomen rises (coughing, sneezing, laughing, lifting) because the pelvic floor and the urethral sphincter are too weak to hold; pregnancy and vaginal delivery stretch and sometimes injure these muscles. Pelvic floor muscle training is the recommended first-line treatment, ahead of surgery, usually for at least three months, and works better when supervised or helped by biofeedback than when learned from a leaflet. It also helps urge incontinence (together with bladder training), the symptoms of pelvic organ prolapse, incontinence in men after prostate surgery, and faecal incontinence. The trap in the options is enuresis. Bed-wetting in a child (enuresis, diagnosable from age 5 in DSM-5) is not a muscle-strength problem. The first-line behavioural treatment is the enuresis alarm, the bell-and-pad of O. Hobart Mowrer and Willie Mae Mowrer (1938): urine completes a circuit, the bell wakes the child, and over weeks a full bladder becomes the cue to wake or hold on, which is classical conditioning with an avoidance component. Desmopressin is the drug option. Dry-bed training (Nathan Azrin and colleagues, 1974) adds scheduled waking, cleanliness training and positive practice to the alarm. So: adult leakage on coughing, Kegels; a child wetting the bed, bell-and-pad.", "traps": {"b": "Childhood bed-wetting is treated first with the enuresis alarm, the bell-and-pad (Mowrer and Mowrer, 1938), or with desmopressin. It is a waking and conditioning problem, not weak pelvic muscles.", "c": "Tics are treated with habit reversal training or CBIT, built on awareness and a competing response. Pelvic floor exercises play no part.", "d": "Insomnia is treated with CBT-I: stimulus control, sleep restriction, sleep hygiene, relaxation and cognitive work. Kegels have no role."}}, {"q": "Arnold Kegel (1948) taught his exercises with a perineometer, a pressure gauge that let the patient watch her own muscle squeeze. This made his method an early form of:", "concept": "The answer is biofeedback: using an instrument to make a bodily process the person cannot normally sense visible or audible, so he can learn to control it. Kegel's perineometer was an air-filled chamber placed in the vagina and connected to a pressure dial. The patient watched the needle rise as she contracted the right muscles, which taught her to find a muscle group most people cannot locate by feel, and let her track her strength as it grew. Biofeedback as a named field came later, in the late 1960s, after Neal Miller's experiments suggested that autonomic responses could be operantly conditioned; the Biofeedback Research Society was founded in 1969. The learning principle is operant: the feedback signal is both information and reinforcement for each small change in the right direction. The main types in clinical psychology: EMG biofeedback for muscle tension (tension headache, pelvic floor training, rehabilitation after stroke); thermal or skin-temperature biofeedback (hand warming for migraine and Raynaud's disease); electrodermal or GSR biofeedback for arousal and anxiety; heart rate variability biofeedback; and EEG biofeedback, called neurofeedback (ADHD, epilepsy). Pelvic floor biofeedback today uses surface EMG or pressure probes and is still a standard part of treating incontinence and some sexual pain problems. Exam pairing: feedback of a body signal is biofeedback; pairing with something unpleasant is aversion therapy; pairing with relaxation is systematic desensitization; tokens exchanged for rewards is a token economy.", "traps": {"a": "Aversion therapy pairs an unwanted behaviour with an unpleasant stimulus. The perineometer gave information, not discomfort.", "b": "A token economy reinforces target behaviours with tokens that are exchanged for rewards (Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin, 1968). No instrument shows a body signal.", "c": "Systematic desensitization pairs feared scenes with relaxation to remove anxiety. Kegel's gauge was about seeing and strengthening a muscle contraction."}}]});
